    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "25-27",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Four wins in a row will soften a lot of noise, and Toronto has finally found some. The club that stumbled through April has turned the corner with a hard, straight blade - <strong>Saturday's 5-2 win over Pittsburgh made it four straight and seven in 10</strong>, the kind of run that changes a clubhouse's mood before it changes the standings. At 25-27, third in the AL East and still 10.5 games back, the Blue Jays aren't pretending to be untouchable; they're just starting to look like a team that can punish mistakes and breathe a little easier. George Springer supplied the sledgehammer again, launching his 65th leadoff homer, while Patrick Corbin gave them the backbone with six strong innings and seven strikeouts. That's the formula now: early punch, clean enough pitching, and no wasted outs. The next test comes quickly and almost unfairly early - <strong>this morning's 11:15 a.m. rematch with the Pirates at Rogers Centre, where Toronto can turn a promising stretch into something more serious</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 24, 2026"
      },
